y.Capabilities[\"filesystem-isolation\"] != \"unavailable\" || f.run.Safety.Capabilities[\"shared-git-isolation\"] != \"unavailable\" {\npose-mcp/internal/pose/governance_read_view.go:1048:\t\top.primary.markUnknown(producer, \"source revision is required for this index build but is unavailable\")\n   118\t\n   119\t### Decision D1\n   120\t- Basis: R1, R2\n   121\tLeftover processes are identified by the sealed-checkout entry the dispatch already sets in the run's environment, not by process group or session: adapters such as `codex exec` run each command in a session of its own, which the group kill misses, and the entry names this run's checkout only.\n   122\t\n   123\t### Decision D2\n   124\t- Basis: R5\n   125\tAn abandoned attempt is closed by the records a failed run writes (a run record and its receipt), released only when its marker is stalled and its owner proven dead. Nothing is deleted from the ledger, and older engines read the result as an ordinary failed run.\n   126\t\n   127\t## 6. Validation\n   128\t\n   129\t### Strategy\n   130\tEach requirement gets a regression test shown failing before its code.\n   131\t\n   132\t### Deterministic checks\n   133\t\n   134\t#### Test\n   135\t- Command: `go test ./...` (in `pose-mcp`), then `pose validate --carry`.\n   136\t\n   137\t### Execution log\n   138\t- 2026-11-24: implemented R1–R6. Each has a test that fails with its fix mutated out: the dispatch ends a process the adapter left in a session of its own (the test's leftover keeps writing into the runtime directory), the release records the failed run and receipt and removes the marker and its stall claim, a live owner is never released, and `review-brief/10` asks commands to finish while `/9` stays unchanged.\n   139\t- 2026-11-24: the containment record disclosed escaped-descendant termination as `unavailable`, and a regression test pinned that limitation. On Linux it is now `environment-marked` (ended while the descendant keeps the run's sealed-checkout entry, never `available`, so a profile that requires the full guarantee is still refused), the limitation text says so, and the test now proves the escaped child is ended with the run.\n   140\t- 2026-11-24: review round 1 (rrn-c5c082703d83c834) requested changes. N1: the reservation was published before the active marker, so a dispatch ended in between left an attempt nothing could release; the dispatch now marks the attempt active first and publishes the reservation after, and a stalled marker without a reservation is removed by the release. When marking itself fails, the dispatch still publishes the reservation and records the failed run and its receipt, keeping the contract of `pose-delegation-result-before-marker`. N2: `delegate ls` now names the abandonment cause and the release command, in text and JSON. N3: each process is pinned by a pidfd and its environment read again through it before SIGKILL, so a reused pid is never signalled. N4: a scan that cannot list `/proc` or read a process state fails; a process of this user whose environment cannot be read is counted and disclosed in the run record rather than skipped (L3). N5: the manual says the reaping is Linux-only. Each fix has a test that fails with it mutated out (N4's disclosure is not reproducible in a test: it needs a non-inspectable process).\n     5\t// Leftover reviewer processes (spec\n     6\t// pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes R1): an adapter such as\n     7\t// `codex exec` runs each command in a session of its own, so killing the\n     8\t// adapter's process group misses a command the reviewer left running, and\n     9\t// that command keeps writing into the run's runtime directory while the\n    10\t// cleanup inventory reads it. Every process of the run inherits\n    11\t// SealedCheckoutEnv naming the run's checkout, so the dispatch finds them by\n    12\t// that entry, ends them and waits for them before the inventory.\n    13\t//\n    14\t// Each process is pinned by a pidfd before it is signalled, and its\n    15\t// environment is read again through the pinned process, so a pid reused by\n    16\t// an unrelated process between the scan and the signal is never killed\n    17\t// (review round 1, N3). A scan that cannot list or read the state of the\n    18\t// processes fails. A process of this user whose environment cannot be read\n    19\t// (one that made itself non-inspectable) cannot be matched: one that already\n    20\t// existed before the adapter started is not the run's and is skipped; any\n    21\t// other may be the run's, so the scan fails and the run stays unconcludable\n    22\t// (review round 2, N2).\n    23\t\n    24\timport (\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
